  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Jack Field",
    "overview": "Third Ear is a switchboard face. Every action is a line: a key with its own cord colour, a three-letter line code printed in a coloured cell, an opal lamp that reports its state, and a printed designation strip that says what happens after the stop. A power user reads the whole board at a glance: which microphone is live, which lines are busy, which have replies waiting, which failed. The category default (a round record button over a waveform, with a recordings list) is refused; recording starts by tapping a line and ends by tapping the line it should go down.\n\nThe face exists in two materials. Dark is bakelite black; light is the warm designation-strip paper itself. Both are flat: depth is a tonal step and a 1 dp rule, never a shadow. Density is whole-cell: keys are full plates with generous targets (136 dp minimum height, 48 dp minimum for every button, 56 dp setting rows) because the audience includes headset and hearing-aid wearers and drivers glancing at a mounted phone.\n\nColour is functional throughout. Cord colours identify lines and nothing else; lamp colours report state and never appear without a word; everything else is ink on face. Motion is a relay, not a fade: two hard frames, no easing.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Line keys in cord colours with code cell, lamp, name, state word and designation strip.",
      "Two flat faces (bakelite and paper), tonal steps and 1 dp rules, no shadows.",
      "Opal lamps with four states, always paired with a word.",
      "Atkinson Hyperlegible Next for everything read; Saira Condensed caps for strips, codes and labels.",
      "One reversed plate per screen and one giant live figure while recording.",
      "Relay-step motion: hard frames, no easing; still when animations are off."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Cord Is Identity Rule",
        "body": "Cord colours mark a line and nothing else: no cord colour as decoration, accent, chart colour or button fill. If it is not a line, it is ink on face.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lamp Speaks Rule",
        "body": "A lit lamp always travels with a word (\"Transcribing\", \"1 reply waiting\", \"Not delivered\"); colour alone never carries state. The same applies to Notices.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The No Dynamic Color Rule",
        "body": "Material Dynamic Color is off. Wallpaper-derived colour would overwrite cord identity; the Material scheme is mapped onto the face palette instead.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Condensed Is Engraving Rule",
        "body": "Saira Condensed appears only in caps, only for short designations (codes, strips, labels, wordmark, timer). Sentences, names and anything read for meaning are Atkinson.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Figures Rule",
        "body": "Numbers that change while visible (timer, durations, counts) use tabular figures so they never jitter.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tonal Step Rule",
        "body": "To separate a surface, step its tone and draw a 1 dp rule. Never a shadow, never a glow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Reversed Plate Rule",
        "body": "At most one plate per screen is reversed into strip colours: the line a recording started on (\"Started here\"). Reversal marks the one thing that is happening, so it is never used for emphasis elsewhere.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do give every action a cord colour, a line code, a lamp and a designation strip wherever it appears (key, tile, widget, notification, car grid).",
      "Do pair every lit lamp and every Notice with a word.",
      "Do separate surfaces with a tonal step and a 1 dp rule; corners are 3 dp (cells) or 6 dp (plates).",
      "Do animate in relay steps: hard frames, no easing (busy lamp 700/300 ms, strip patch flash 90 ms), and hold lamps lit when animations are off.",
      "Do keep targets at least 48 dp and setting rows at least 56 dp; drop the Board to one column at font scale 1.3 and above.",
      "Do cap lists and settings at a 600 dp reading measure on wide screens while the Board takes the full width.",
      "Do use tabular figures for anything that counts while visible."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use a cord colour for anything but a line's identity.",
      "Don't turn on Dynamic Color.",
      "Don't add shadows, glows or Material tonal elevation.",
      "Don't show an idle level meter on the input bar; the microphone stays closed until recording.",
      "Don't put search or a menu on the Board; search lives in Log, settings in Setup.",
      "Don't reverse more than one plate per screen.",
      "Don't set sentences or names in Saira Condensed, or Saira in lower case."
    ]
  }
}